{"id":"3e814193-4559-461c-a986-bf39dea601ae","object":"chat.completion","created":1784825947,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import collections\\ndef solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        node_by_id[n['id']] = n\\n    children = collections.defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n    if 'root' not in node_by_id:\\n        raise ValueError('Root node missing')\\n    root = node_by_id['root']\\n    if root['parent'] is not None:\\n        raise ValueError('Root has parent')\\n    if root['symbol'] != 'root':\\n        raise ValueError('Root symbol is not root')\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None and n['parent'] not in node_by_id:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Parent {n['parent']} not found\\\")\\n    symbols_present = set(n['symbol'] for n in nodes)\\n    for rs in required_symbols:\\n        if rs not in symbols_present:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{rs}' not present\\\")\\n    present = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in present:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in present for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        present.remove(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(present)\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that performs grammar-guided node reduction. The case contains nodes (with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility). required_symbols list. We need to output retained IDs and removal order. The process: we have a tree. We need to reduce by removing removable nodes, but preserving that all required symbols remain represented (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains, not necessarily the root? The root is always present and its symbol \"root\" is in required_symbols). Also we must not remove root, not remove required symbol nodes (but some nodes with required symbols might be removable? In fixtures, root is not removable, but n1 has symbol \"s1\" which is required, but n1 is not removable. However there could be cases where a required symbol node is removable? The problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we cannot remove it even if removable flag is true? That is a hard constraint. So we must first filter out nodes that are not removable, or if their symbol is required, treat as not removable.\n\nAlso we must consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes (depth descending). For ties same depth, use node ID as stable tie break. Tentatively attempt removal: check if after removal, all required symbols still have at least one node present (in the remaining tree). Also ensure grammar validity? The problem mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but in the context of Perses. However the fixture doesn't provide grammar rules. The task: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" but we only have tree with symbols. The bounded objective says \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" Interestingness oracle is not provided. Possibly the \"interestingness_preserved\" is a property that we need to assume always true? Wait: \"Validate every required field before computing a result. ... Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth. ... Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" There's also mention of \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\" as hard contracts. But we don't have a function to check grammar validity. Perhaps the fixture is just a test, and we don't need to enforce grammar validity beyond the removal constraints? The task says \"Implement grammar_guided_node_reduction through solve(case) for this bounded objective: Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But the actual input only contains nodes and required_symbols. It seems the \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness\" checks are to be treated as always satisfied? Or maybe the reduction algorithm should only consider removable nodes and preserve required symbols. The \"interestingness\" might be defined by whether all required symbols remain. I think the core is: we have a tree, we want to remove as many removable nodes as possible while ensuring that for each required symbol, there is at least one node in the remaining tree with that symbol. Also we cannot remove nodes that have children that are retained (i.e., if node has children that are not removed, then we cannot remove the parent? Actually \"a node with retained children\" means if any child of that node is retained (not removed), then we cannot remove the parent, because that would orphan the child? But in tree reduction, if you remove a node, its children become children of the parent? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed), then removing that node would cause the child to be disconnected (since parent removed). In proper tree reduction, typically when you remove a node, you attach its children to its parent (or something). But here the constraint says don't remove a node that has retained children. So we must only remove leaf nodes? Or nodes whose children are all already removed? Actually the algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" This suggests we process nodes from deepest to shallowest. At each step, we consider removing the node if it is removable, not required, and all its descendants? But the constraint about retained children: if we process deepest nodes first, then when we consider a parent, its children might already have been removed (if they were removable and passed checks). So the parent might have no retained children left. So we can remove it if all its children were already removed. Also we must ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree. So the algorithm: Build tree structure. Build mapping from symbol to list of nodes with that symbol. Determine which nodes are initially present. Then iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending (for tie). For each node that is still present, if it is not removable (removable == false) or its symbol is required, skip. Also if it has any child that is currently present (i.e., retained), skip. Then simulate removal: temporarily remove node, check if for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol still present (excluding root? root always present). If condition holds, then actually remove node: mark it removed, update child-parent relationships? But since we only care about retained children, we need to maintain a set of present nodes. For checking if a node has retained children, we need to know if any child is present. So we can maintain a set of present node IDs. Initially all nodes present (except maybe we don't consider root as removable). Then iterate. When we remove a node, we remove it from present set. Also we might need to update the parent's children count or something. For the \"retained children\" check: we need to see if any child of this node is still in present set. So we need a parent-child mapping. We have parent field. Build children list per node. Then for a node, its children are nodes whose parent equals this node's id. So to check retained children, we can iterate through children and see if any child id is in present set. If yes, cannot remove. After removal, we remove node from present set. No need to update children because they will be checked later? But note that if we remove a node, its children become orphaned? But the constraint says we cannot remove a node with retained children, so we will only remove nodes whose children are already removed. So after removal, children are already absent. So that's fine.\n\nWe also need to consider that we might have multiple nodes with same symbol. Required symbols must remain represented. That means after removal, there must exist at least one node with each required symbol. So we can precompute a count of nodes per symbol initially. Then when we remove a node, decrement count for its symbol. If removal would cause count to become zero for any required symbol, we cannot remove it. So we need to maintain symbol counts for required symbols only? Or all? Actually we only care about required symbols. So we can have a dict required_counts: {symbol: count of present nodes with that symbol}. Initially from all nodes. Then during removal simulation, we check if after decrement, all required symbols have count >=1. If yes, proceed (and actually decrement). If not, skip.\n\nAlso note that root is always present, symbol \"root\". The required_symbols list includes \"root\" and \"s1\". So we must ensure at least one \"s1\" remains. In fixtures, n1 has \"s1\" and is not removable, so fine. But if there were multiple s1 nodes and some removable, we could remove some as long as at least one remains.\n\nNow, what about \"grammar_valid\"? The problem statement says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" But we have no grammar. Perhaps we should assume the input is always grammar-valid. The task is to implement the reduction algorithm as described. The output should be: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The return should be a JSON-serializable object. Based on the instruction: \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So we need to decide what to return. The candidate_artifact says: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So likely we return a dict with keys \"retained_ids\" (list of IDs sorted) and \"removal_order\" (list of IDs in order they were removed). Also maybe other fields? The initial description: \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision, and compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\" But the main output is retained_ids and removal_order. I'll output exactly those two keys.\n\nWe must not mutate case. Raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. What constitutes infeasible? If required symbol has zero nodes initially? Or if root is missing? We can validate: there must be a node with id \"root\" and parent null and symbol \"root\" (assume). Also required_symbols must be subset of symbols present? If any required symbol not present initially, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso we need to ensure that the tree structure is valid: each node's parent exists except root, depth consistent? We can ignore depth maybe. But we'll do basic validation.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n- Parse case['payload']: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n- Build mapping from node id to node dict.\n- Build children list per node.\n- Validate: root node exists, parent null, symbol 'root'. All nodes have parent that exists except root. required_symbols all present in at least one node.\n- present = set of all node ids initially.\n- For each required symbol, maintain count: dict req_sym_counts = {sym: count of nodes with that symbol initially}.\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending.\n- removal_order = []\n- For each node in sorted order:\n  - if node id not in present: continue (already removed)\n  - if node['removable'] is False: continue\n  - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue (since required symbol nodes cannot be removed, even if removable flag true? The constraint says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So yes, we cannot remove a node whose symbol is required. So skip.\n  - Check retained children: for child_id in children[node_id]: if child_id in present: cannot remove, continue.\n  - Simulate: new_counts = req_sym_counts.copy(); new_counts[node['symbol']] -= 1; if new_counts[node['symbol']] < 0: should not happen. If any required symbol count becomes 0: cannot remove.\n  - Else: perform removal: remove node from present; update req_sym_counts = new_counts; removal_order.append(node_id).\n- After loop, retained_ids = sorted(present) (by id? or as they appear? The instruction says \"sorted retained IDs\". So sorted alphabetically/numerically? IDs are strings like 'root','n1','n2'... Sorting lexicographically is fine. But 'root' should be first? Sorting strings: 'n1','n2',...,'root'? Typically lex order: 'n1' < 'n2' < 'root' because 'r' > 'n'. That might be acceptable but might break expected output. The fixture examples don't show expected output. However the instruction says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" For output \"sorted retained IDs\" likely they want sorted in ascending order by ID as strings. We'll do that.\n- Return {\"retained_ids\": sorted(present), \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nBut wait: The process described might also allow replacement candidates? The problem mentions \"deletion and replacement candidates\" but no replacement logic given. We'll ignore replacement as it's not in input.\n\nAlso check: The nodes have utility field. Not used in constraints. But maybe interestingness is based on utility? But the specification says \"interestingness_preserved\" as a hard contract. Without oracle, we assume always true as long as required symbols remain. So we'll ignore utility.\n\nNow, we must encode the solution as JSON with key \"implementation\" containing Python source code as a string. We'll write the function solve(case). We'll ensure it raises ValueError on invalid.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nEdge cases: If a node has removable=True but symbol is required, we skip. If a node has removable=False but symbol not required, skip (cannot remove non-removable). If a node has removable=True and symbol not required and no retained children, proceed.\n\nPotential issue: After removing a node, its children might become orphaned? But we only remove nodes that have no retained children, so children are already removed. So orphaned children are already absent. So the parent removal is safe.\n\nBut what if a node has a child that is not removable? Then that child is retained, so we cannot remove the parent. That is correct.\n\nNow, the order of processing: deeper nodes first. That ensures leaf nodes are considered before their parents. So after removing leaf nodes, parent may become removable if all its children are gone.\n\nNow, the algorithm as described should produce a deterministic result.\n\nNow, output format: The function must return a JSON-serializable object. In Python, returning a dict with lists works. But the problem says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" Possibly they expect the dict with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". I'll go with that.\n\nNow, write the solve function.\n\nLet's also handle case where there are multiple roots? Assume only one root.\n\nWe'll also consider that nodes may not be in a tree order? But parent field ensures tree structure.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Input case is a dict with 'payload' key.\n- nodes = case['payload']['nodes']\n- required_symbols = case['payload']['required_symbols']\n\nWe'll convert to list of dicts.\n\nWrite code.\n\nNow, output as JSON string with escaping. I'll produce the final answer.\n\nBut need to ensure no imports beyond standard library. We'll use collections.defaultdict for children mapping, but standard lib without import? We can use dict and manually .get. Or import collections - that's allowed. We'll import collections.\n\nAlso, we'll need to sort nodes by depth descending and id ascending. We'll use sorted with key lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']). Assuming depth is integer. IDs are strings, so sorted lexicographically. That's fine.\n\nNow, produce final JSON.\n\nI'll write the code as:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\n    # Build dicts\n    node_by_id = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        node_by_id[n['id']] = n\n    children = collections.defaultdict(list)\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    # Validate\n    if 'root' not in node_by_id:\n        raise ValueError(\"Root node missing\")\n    root = node_by_id['root']\n    if root['parent'] is not None:\n        raise ValueError(\"Root has parent\")\n    if root['symbol'] != 'root':\n        raise ValueError(\"Root symbol not 'root'\")\n    # Check all parents exist\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is not None and n['parent'] not in node_by_id:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Parent {n['parent']} not found\")\n    # Check required symbols exist\n    symbols_present = set(n['symbol'] for n in nodes)\n    for rs in required_symbols:\n        if rs not in symbols_present:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{rs}' not present\")\n    # Initial present set\n    present = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    # Symbol counts for required symbols only\n    req_counts = {sym: 0 for sym in required_symbols}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            req_counts[n['symbol']] += 1\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in present:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in present for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Simulate removal\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        new_counts = req_counts.copy()\n        new_counts[sym] -= 1\n        if sym in required_symbols and new_counts[sym] <= 0:\n            continue  # would remove last required symbol\n        # Perform removal\n        present.remove(nid)\n        req_counts = new_counts\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted(present)\n    return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nBut note: when we decrement new_counts[sym] -= 1, we only care if sym is required. But we are already skipping if symbol is required, so sym will not be in required_symbols at that point. Wait, we have condition \"if n['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue\", so we never process nodes with required symbols. So in the simulation, sym is not required. So we don't need to check required symbol counts for that node. However, there is a scenario: a node with a required symbol might be removable according to flag, but we are required not to remove it. So we skip it. So the simulation step is only for non-required nodes. So we don't need to copy and decrement required counts for non-required symbols. Actually the constraint \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" For a non-required node, removing it does not affect required symbols count. So the check is trivial: always true. So we can skip the simulation for non-required nodes. But careful: if a node's symbol is not required, but removing it might affect the presence of required symbols? No, only its own symbol matters. So we don't need to check. So we can remove the simulation. However, what if the node's removal could cause a required symbol to become absent because the node is the only carrier of that symbol? But if its symbol is not required, no issue. So we can just remove it. But wait, there is subtlety: required symbols are symbols that must exist, not nodes. So as long as at least one node with that symbol remains, it's fine. So removing a non-required node does not affect that. So yes, no need for counts. So we can simplify: for non-required nodes, we just check removable and no retained children, then remove. But we must still ensure that we never remove a node that causes a required symbol to disappear. That can only happen if we remove a node with a required symbol, but we already skip those. So we can simplify.\n\nBut the instruction says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That could be a general condition, but for non-required nodes it's automatically satisfied. So we can implement generic check: if all required symbols remain represented after removal. But since we don't need to compute for non-required, we can just proceed. I'll include the generic check for correctness, but it's redundant.\n\nHowever, consider a scenario where a node has a non-required symbol, but it is the only node that has a certain structure? No.\n\nSo I'll keep the code simple: skip nodes with required symbol, then remove if no retained children.\n\nBut there is also the possibility that a node's symbol is not required, but its removal might cause the root's symbol to be lost? Root's symbol \"root\" is required, but we never remove root. So fine.\n\nNow, do we need to also consider that after removing a node, its parent might become removable? That's handled by the depth-first ordering.\n\nNow, about grammar_valid and interestingness: we ignore.\n\nBut the problem statement says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" We validate tree structure and required symbols.\n\nNow, return value: exact named fields \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". The JSON serializable object.\n\nNow, we must ensure we use only Python standard library. collections.defaultdict is ok.\n\nNow, produce final JSON with single key \"implementation\" containing Python source as string.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and quotes.\n\nWe'll write the code in a multiline string, then escape.\n\nLet's produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1849,"completion_tokens":4889,"total_tokens":6738,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4419},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":57},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}